# Blog articles. Practical, general advice — no invented statistics.

ARTICLES = [
dict(slug="business-website-essentials", cat="Guide", folder="Websites", mins="7 min", img="desk", img2="code",
 title="How much does a business website really need?",
 desc="The pages, features and content a small business website needs to bring enquiries — and the extras you can safely skip.",
 intro="Most small business owners in Delhi NCR don't need a huge website. They need a clear, fast one that answers a customer's questions in under a minute and makes it easy to call or WhatsApp. Here's exactly what to include — and what you can skip until later.",
 sections=[
  ("Start with the goal, not the pages", """<p>Before deciding how many pages you need, decide what a visitor should do. For most local businesses the answer is simple: <strong>call, send a WhatsApp message or fill an enquiry form</strong>. Every page should push gently towards that one action.</p>
<p>A clinic wants appointment requests. A coaching institute wants admission enquiries. A manufacturer wants quotation requests. Write that goal down — it will decide your layout, your buttons and even the words on your home page.</p>"""),
  ("The five pages almost every business needs", """<ol>
<li><strong>Home</strong> — who you are, what you do, where you serve, and a clear button to contact you. Visitors should understand this in five seconds.</li>
<li><strong>Services</strong> — one page per main service is ideal for Google. Each page should explain the service, who it's for, the process and common questions.</li>
<li><strong>About</strong> — real photos of you, your team and your workplace. People buy from people they trust.</li>
<li><strong>Work / gallery</strong> — photos of finished work, before-and-after shots or project examples.</li>
<li><strong>Contact</strong> — phone, WhatsApp, email, address, Google Map and a short form.</li>
</ol>
<p>If you serve several cities or areas, add a page for each area too. That's one of the simplest ways to show up when people search “service + area name”.</p>"""),
  ("Features that actually bring enquiries", """<ul>
<li><strong>Click-to-call and WhatsApp buttons</strong> visible on every page, especially on mobile.</li>
<li><strong>Short enquiry forms</strong> — name, phone and one question is enough. Long forms get abandoned.</li>
<li><strong>Trust signals</strong> — real reviews, certifications, years in business, recognisable photos.</li>
<li><strong>Fast loading</strong> — compressed images and good hosting. Slow sites lose visitors before they even see your offer.</li>
<li><strong>Google Map and address</strong> on the contact page, matching your Google Business Profile exactly.</li>
</ul>"""),
  ("What you can skip (for now)", """<p>A blog, multiple languages, a customer login area, animations everywhere, a chatbot — these can all be useful later, but none of them matter if the basics are weak. Launch a solid core site first, measure what visitors do, then add features that solve real problems.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Tip:</b> If you're unsure whether you need something, ask: “Will this help a visitor decide to contact me?” If the answer is no, park it.</div>"""),
  ("Content matters more than design", """<p>A beautiful website with vague text converts poorly. Write the way your customers speak. Answer the questions they ask you on the phone every day: price range, timelines, process, location, guarantees. Use headings so people can scan, and keep paragraphs short.</p>
<p>Good content also helps Google understand your pages. Mention your services and the areas you serve naturally — no keyword stuffing.</p>"""),
  ("Ownership and maintenance", """<p>Always make sure the domain, hosting and website are registered in <strong>your</strong> name or your company's name. Ask for admin access and backups. Plan for small updates every month — new photos, offers, fixing broken links — so the site never looks abandoned.</p>"""),
 ],
 takeaways=["Decide the one action you want visitors to take","Home, Services, About, Work and Contact cover most businesses","Make calling and WhatsApp one tap on mobile","Clear content beats fancy design","Own your domain, hosting and code"],
 faq=[("How many pages should a small business website have?","Most local businesses do well with 5–10 pages: home, a page per main service, about, work/gallery and contact, plus area pages if you serve several locations."),("Do I need a blog?","Not on day one. A blog helps SEO over time, but get your core pages right first."),("How long does it take to build?","A focused business website usually takes 1–2 weeks once content and photos are ready.")]),

dict(slug="rank-on-google-maps", cat="Local SEO", folder="Google Maps", mins="6 min", img="phone", img2="india_gate",
 title="7 ways to rank your shop on Google Maps",
 desc="Simple, practical Google Business Profile steps that help local businesses in Delhi NCR appear in Google Maps and “near me” searches.",
 intro="When someone searches “dentist near me” or “bakery in Indirapuram”, Google shows a map with three businesses first. Getting into that list can bring more calls than any website. Here are seven steps that make a real difference.",
 sections=[
  ("1. Claim and verify your Google Business Profile", """<p>Search for your business on Google. If a profile exists, claim it; if not, create one. Verification proves you own the business and unlocks editing, posts, photos and insights. Without verification, you can't manage how you appear.</p>"""),
  ("2. Choose the right primary category", """<p>Your <strong>primary category</strong> is one of the strongest signals Google uses. Be specific: “Dental clinic” is better than “Doctor”, “Cake shop” better than “Shop”. Add secondary categories only if they truly describe what you do.</p>"""),
  ("3. Keep name, address and phone consistent", """<p>Your business name, address and phone number should match exactly on your website, Google profile, Facebook, Justdial and other listings. Small differences (like “Shop 12” vs “S-12”) confuse search engines and customers.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Tip:</b> Use the same phone number everywhere and make sure it is answered — missed calls hurt your business more than your ranking.</div>"""),
  ("4. Add real photos regularly", """<p>Upload photos of your storefront, interiors, team and work. Real photos help customers recognise you and make the profile look active. Avoid only using stock images; add new photos every few weeks.</p>"""),
  ("5. Collect reviews the right way", """<p>Ask every happy customer for a review — the best moment is right after a good experience. Send your review link on WhatsApp so it's one tap. <strong>Never buy fake reviews</strong>: they can get your profile suspended and customers notice them.</p>
<p>Reply to every review, good or bad. A calm, helpful reply to a complaint often wins more trust than a perfect rating.</p>"""),
  ("6. Fill every section and post updates", """<p>Add services, products, opening hours (including holidays), attributes and a clear description. Use Google posts for offers, events and new arrivals. A complete, active profile signals that you're open and relevant.</p>"""),
  ("7. Connect a fast, local website", """<p>Link your profile to a website page that matches what people searched for. A page that mentions your services and your area — for example “Physiotherapy in Vaishali” — helps Google connect the dots. Make sure it loads fast on mobile and has a clear call button.</p>"""),
 ],
 takeaways=["Claim and verify your profile first","Pick the most specific primary category","Keep name, address and phone identical everywhere","Add real photos and ask for genuine reviews","Link to a fast, local landing page"],
 faq=[("How long does it take to rank on Google Maps?","It varies by competition and location. Profile improvements can help within weeks; strong positions usually take a few months of consistent effort."),("Can I rank in areas where I don't have an address?","Google mostly shows businesses close to the searcher. Service-area businesses can define areas they serve, but distance still matters."),("Should I pay for reviews?","No. Fake or paid reviews break Google's policies and can get your profile suspended.")]),

dict(slug="crm-stop-losing-leads", cat="CRM", folder="Leads", mins="7 min", img="laptop_mug", img2="data",
 title="Why small businesses lose leads — and how a CRM fixes it",
 desc="Missed calls, forgotten follow-ups and lost WhatsApp chats cost businesses customers every week. Here's how a simple CRM stops the leak.",
 intro="Most businesses don't have a lead problem — they have a follow-up problem. Enquiries come from calls, WhatsApp, Instagram, the website and walk-ins, and they end up scattered across phones, notebooks and memory. A CRM puts them in one place so nobody slips through.",
 sections=[
  ("Where leads usually get lost", """<ul>
<li><strong>Missed calls</strong> that nobody calls back.</li>
<li><strong>WhatsApp chats</strong> that get buried under new messages.</li>
<li><strong>“Call me next week”</strong> promises with no reminder.</li>
<li><strong>Staff changes</strong> — when someone leaves, their leads leave with them.</li>
<li><strong>No status</strong> — nobody knows who is interested, who got a quote and who said no.</li>
</ul>"""),
  ("What a CRM actually does", """<p>A CRM (Customer Relationship Management) system is simply an organised list of every enquiry with its <strong>stage</strong>, <strong>next follow-up date</strong> and <strong>history</strong>. Each morning your team opens it and sees exactly who to call today.</p>
<p>Typical stages look like: New → Contacted → Interested → Meeting scheduled → Quote sent → Negotiation → Won / Lost. You can name them to match how you sell.</p>"""),
  ("The features that matter most", """<ol>
<li><strong>Follow-up reminders</strong> — today's and overdue tasks on one screen.</li>
<li><strong>Lead source tracking</strong> — know whether a lead came from Google, Instagram, referral or ads.</li>
<li><strong>Notes and history</strong> — every call and message recorded against the client.</li>
<li><strong>Payments and renewals</strong> — what's been paid, what's due and when plans renew.</li>
<li><strong>Mobile access</strong> — because most follow-ups happen from a phone.</li>
</ol>
<div class="tip"><b>From our experience:</b> we run GrowVika on a CRM we built ourselves. The single biggest improvement was simply seeing “who to call today” every morning.</div>"""),
  ("Ready-made or custom CRM?", """<p>Ready-made tools are quick to start but often charge per user, include features you'll never use, and force their process on you. A custom CRM costs more upfront but fits your exact workflow, has no per-user fees and you own the data. For small teams with a clear process, a focused custom CRM is often the better long-term choice.</p>"""),
  ("How to start without overwhelm", """<p>Start small: put every new enquiry into the CRM for one month, with a stage and a next follow-up date. Import your old Excel sheet if you have one. Once the habit sticks, add payments, renewals and reports.</p>"""),
 ],
 takeaways=["Leads are usually lost in follow-up, not in marketing","Every lead needs a stage and a next follow-up date","Track the source so you know which marketing works","Mobile access is essential for busy teams","Start simple and build the habit first"],
 faq=[("Is a CRM only for big companies?","No. Even a one-person business benefits from never forgetting a follow-up."),("Can I import my Excel leads?","Yes. Most CRMs, including custom ones we build, can import from Excel or Google Sheets."),("Will my team actually use it?","They will if it's simple and fits how they already work — that's the main advantage of a custom CRM.")]),

dict(slug="app-or-website", cat="Apps", folder="Mobile apps", mins="6 min", img="mobile", img2="phone",
 title="Do you need an app, or just a better website?",
 desc="A simple way to decide whether your business needs a mobile app or whether a fast, mobile-friendly website is enough.",
 intro="“Should we build an app?” is one of the most common questions we hear. Sometimes the honest answer is no — a fast mobile website does the job at a fraction of the cost. Here's how to decide.",
 sections=[
  ("Start with how often customers come back", """<p>Apps make sense when customers use your service <strong>repeatedly</strong> — ordering food every week, booking classes, tracking deliveries, checking accounts. If customers visit once or twice a year (like a lawyer or an interior designer), a website is almost always enough.</p>"""),
  ("When a website is the better choice", """<ul>
<li>You mainly need people to find you on Google and contact you.</li>
<li>Customers don't need to log in or save anything.</li>
<li>You want something live within a couple of weeks.</li>
<li>Your budget is limited and you'd rather invest in marketing.</li>
</ul>"""),
  ("When an app is worth it", """<ul>
<li><strong>Repeat orders or bookings</strong> — restaurants, salons, gyms, clinics.</li>
<li><strong>Loyalty and memberships</strong> — points, plans, renewals.</li>
<li><strong>Push notifications</strong> — reminders and offers that bring people back.</li>
<li><strong>Device features</strong> — location tracking, camera, offline use.</li>
<li><strong>Field teams</strong> — sales or service staff updating work from the road.</li>
</ul>"""),
  ("The middle path: a web app", """<p>A progressive web app (PWA) runs in the browser but can be added to the home screen like an app. It's cheaper than native apps and works on Android and iPhone. It's a great first step to test whether customers really want an app.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Tip:</b> If you do build an app, build Android and iOS from one codebase (for example Flutter or React Native) so you don't pay twice.</div>"""),
  ("Don't forget the running costs", """<p>Apps need updates for new phone versions, store fees, hosting and ongoing fixes. Budget for monthly maintenance, not just the first build.</p>"""),
 ],
 takeaways=["Repeat usage is the main reason to build an app","A fast mobile website is enough for most local businesses","A PWA is a low-cost middle path","Build both platforms from one codebase","Plan for ongoing maintenance"],
 faq=[("How much does an app cost compared to a website?","Apps usually cost several times more than a business website because of the admin panel, two platforms and store publishing. We quote after understanding features."),("Can my website become an app later?","Yes. Many businesses start with a website or PWA and build an app once demand is proven."),("How long does an app take?","A focused first version typically takes 4–8 weeks.")]),

dict(slug="reels-that-bring-customers", cat="Social", folder="Instagram", mins="6 min", img="social", img2="marketing",
 title="Reels that bring customers, not just likes",
 desc="What local businesses in Delhi NCR should post on Instagram, how often, and how to turn views into enquiries.",
 intro="Views and likes feel good, but they don't pay rent. The goal of social media for a local business is simple: build trust so that when someone needs you, they message you. Here's a practical plan.",
 sections=[
  ("Post what customers want to see", """<ul>
<li><strong>Behind the scenes</strong> — your kitchen, workshop, clinic or team at work.</li>
<li><strong>Before and after</strong> — transformations sell services better than any caption.</li>
<li><strong>Answers to common questions</strong> — one question per reel, answered in 20–30 seconds.</li>
<li><strong>Real customers</strong> (with permission) — short reactions and stories.</li>
<li><strong>Offers and new arrivals</strong> — but not every day.</li>
</ul>"""),
  ("Consistency beats virality", """<p>Three good posts a week, every week, will do more than one viral reel followed by silence. Plan a simple monthly calendar and batch-shoot content in one or two sessions.</p>"""),
  ("Make it easy to contact you", """<p>Add WhatsApp and call buttons to your profile, a clear bio with your area (“Bakery in Raj Nagar, Ghaziabad”) and a link to a page on your website. End reels with a simple call to action: “DM ‘MENU’ for prices” or “Tap WhatsApp to book”.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Tip:</b> Reply to comments and DMs quickly. A slow reply often means a lost customer.</div>"""),
  ("Measure what matters", """<p>Track profile visits, WhatsApp clicks, DMs and calls — not just views. If a type of post brings enquiries, make more of it.</p>"""),
  ("When to add paid promotion", """<p>Boost posts that already perform well organically, targeted to your area and age group. Small, focused budgets on proven content are better than large budgets on untested ideas.</p>"""),
 ],
 takeaways=["Post to build trust, not just views","Behind-the-scenes and before/after content work well","Consistency beats one viral reel","Make WhatsApp and calling one tap","Measure enquiries, not likes"],
 faq=[("How often should a local business post?","Around 3–4 times a week is a good, sustainable target for most businesses."),("Do I need professional video?","No. Clear phone videos with good light and sound work well for local businesses."),("Should I run ads?","Yes, once you know which posts bring enquiries — then promote those to your local area.")]),

dict(slug="google-ads-vs-meta-ads", cat="Ads", folder="Google & Meta", mins="7 min", img="marketing", img2="data",
 title="Google Ads vs Meta Ads for local businesses",
 desc="Where local businesses should spend first — Google search ads or Facebook and Instagram ads — and how to know it's working.",
 intro="Both Google Ads and Meta (Facebook and Instagram) Ads can bring customers, but they work very differently. Google captures people who are already searching. Meta reaches people who aren't searching yet. Here's how to choose.",
 sections=[
  ("Google Ads: catch people who are ready", """<p>When someone types “AC repair in Dwarka” they need it now. Google search ads put you at the top for these searches. They work best for <strong>urgent or high-intent services</strong>: repairs, clinics, legal help, coaching admissions, B2B suppliers.</p>"""),
  ("Meta Ads: create demand and build trust", """<p>Facebook and Instagram ads appear while people scroll. They're great for <strong>visual products and offers</strong>: food, fashion, salons, events, real-estate projects and lead forms. They also build familiarity, so people recognise you later.</p>"""),
  ("Which should you start with?", """<ul>
<li>If people <strong>search</strong> for what you sell → start with Google.</li>
<li>If your product is <strong>visual or impulse-driven</strong> → start with Meta.</li>
<li>If you have a small budget → pick one, learn, then expand.</li>
</ul>
<div class="tip"><b>Tip:</b> Whatever you choose, send traffic to a page made for that ad — not just your home page.</div>"""),
  ("Track leads, not clicks", """<p>Set up conversion tracking for calls, WhatsApp clicks and form submissions. Then you can see your <strong>cost per lead</strong> and, with a CRM, your cost per customer. Without tracking, you're guessing.</p>"""),
  ("Common mistakes to avoid", """<ol>
<li>Targeting all of Delhi NCR when you only serve one area.</li>
<li>Sending ads to a slow or confusing page.</li>
<li>Changing everything every two days — give campaigns time to learn.</li>
<li>Not answering leads quickly.</li>
</ol>"""),
 ],
 takeaways=["Google Ads capture existing demand","Meta Ads create demand and build trust","Start with one platform and learn","Use dedicated landing pages","Track cost per lead and per customer"],
 faq=[("What budget should I start with?","Start with a small test budget for a few weeks, measure cost per lead, then scale what works."),("Can I run both together?","Yes. Many businesses use Google for ready buyers and Meta for awareness and retargeting."),("How soon will I get leads?","Ads can bring enquiries within days of launch, though results improve as campaigns are optimised.")]),

dict(slug="shopify-vs-woocommerce", cat="E-commerce", folder="Online store", mins="7 min", img="shop", img2="phone",
 title="Shopify vs WooCommerce for Indian brands",
 desc="A plain-English comparison of Shopify and WooCommerce for Indian businesses — costs, payments, shipping and which to choose.",
 intro="Shopify and WooCommerce are the two most popular ways to build an online store. Both can work well for Indian brands. The right choice depends on your budget, catalogue and how much control you want.",
 sections=[
  ("Shopify in short", """<p>Shopify is a hosted platform: you pay a monthly subscription and Shopify handles hosting, security and updates. It's quick to set up, very reliable and has a large app store. You trade some flexibility and ongoing app costs for convenience.</p>"""),
  ("WooCommerce in short", """<p>WooCommerce is a free plugin for WordPress. You pay for hosting, theme and any premium plugins. It gives you more control over design and features and no platform subscription — but you're responsible for hosting, updates and security.</p>"""),
  ("Payments and shipping in India", """<p>Both platforms support popular Indian payment gateways for UPI, cards, wallets and net banking, and both can offer cash on delivery. Shipping aggregators like Shiprocket integrate with both for courier booking and tracking. Check gateway fees and settlement times before deciding.</p>"""),
  ("How to choose", """<ul>
<li><strong>Choose Shopify</strong> if you want to launch fast, prefer less technical maintenance and are fine with monthly fees.</li>
<li><strong>Choose WooCommerce</strong> if you already use WordPress, want deep customisation or want to avoid platform subscriptions.</li>
<li><strong>Consider custom</strong> if your business has unusual workflows — B2B pricing, multi-vendor, subscriptions or complex integrations.</li>
</ul>
<div class="tip"><b>Tip:</b> Whichever you pick, make sure your domain and store data are in your name so you can move later.</div>"""),
  ("What matters more than the platform", """<p>Good product photos, clear prices, honest delivery times, easy returns and fast mobile checkout matter far more than the platform. Plan your marketing — Meta catalogue ads, Google Shopping and WhatsApp — from day one.</p>"""),
 ],
 takeaways=["Shopify is faster to launch with less maintenance","WooCommerce offers more control without subscriptions","Both support Indian payments, COD and shipping","Custom builds suit unusual workflows","Photos, trust and checkout matter most"],
 faq=[("Which is cheaper?","WooCommerce has no subscription but needs hosting and plugins; Shopify has monthly fees but less maintenance. Total cost depends on your needs."),("Can I move from one to the other later?","Yes, products and customers can be migrated, though it takes planning."),("Do both support cash on delivery?","Yes, both can offer COD alongside online payments.")]),

dict(slug="replace-spreadsheets", cat="Software", folder="Automation", mins="6 min", img="screen", img2="sketch",
 title="5 spreadsheets your business should replace",
 desc="Billing, stock, attendance, bookings and leads — when a small custom tool saves more time than another Excel sheet.",
 intro="Spreadsheets are brilliant for getting started. But when several people edit the same file, copy data between sheets or send screenshots on WhatsApp, mistakes creep in. These five are usually the first worth replacing.",
 sections=[
  ("1. Billing and invoices", """<p>Manually creating invoices in Excel leads to duplicate numbers, wrong totals and missing GST details. A billing tool generates invoices, tracks what's paid and what's due, and gives you clean reports for your accountant.</p>"""),
  ("2. Stock and inventory", """<p>If stock is updated at the end of the day — or forgotten — you oversell or run out. A simple inventory system updates stock with every sale or purchase and alerts you when items run low.</p>"""),
  ("3. Staff attendance", """<p>Attendance sheets get edited after the fact and are hard to total at month-end. A small attendance app with check-in/check-out and monthly summaries saves arguments and time.</p>"""),
  ("4. Bookings and appointments", """<p>Double bookings happen when calendars live in multiple places. A booking system shows available slots, sends reminders and lets customers book themselves.</p>"""),
  ("5. Leads and follow-ups", """<p>A lead sheet can't remind you to call someone. A CRM can — and it shows the history of every conversation so anyone in the team can pick it up.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Tip:</b> Don't replace everything at once. Start with the sheet that causes the most daily pain.</div>"""),
  ("When custom software is worth it", """<p>If a task takes hours every week, involves several people, or mistakes cost money, a focused custom tool usually pays for itself. Build the smallest useful version first, then improve it based on how your team actually uses it.</p>"""),
 ],
 takeaways=["Shared spreadsheets cause errors as teams grow","Billing, stock, attendance, bookings and leads are common first targets","Start with the biggest daily pain","Build a small useful version first","You should own the software and data"],
 faq=[("Is custom software expensive?","A focused tool for one job can be affordable. We quote a fixed price for the first version."),("Can it import my existing data?","Yes, data from Excel or Google Sheets can usually be imported."),("Will it work on phones?","Yes, we build browser-based tools that work on desktop and mobile.")]),

dict(slug="website-slow-on-mobile", cat="Websites", folder="Speed", mins="6 min", img="code", img2="desk",
 title="Why your website is slow on mobile (and how to fix it)",
 desc="Large images, heavy themes, cheap hosting and too many plugins — the common reasons websites are slow on phones and how to fix them.",
 intro="Most of your visitors are on phones, often on mobile data. If your site takes too long to load, many will leave before they see what you offer. The good news: the usual causes are fixable.",
 sections=[
  ("1. Images that are far too big", """<p>Photos straight from a camera or phone can be several megabytes each. Resize them to the size they're displayed at and compress them (WebP or optimised JPEG). This alone often makes the biggest difference.</p>"""),
  ("2. Heavy themes and page builders", """<p>Multipurpose themes load code for features you don't use. Lightweight themes or custom-built pages load only what's needed.</p>"""),
  ("3. Too many plugins and scripts", """<p>Every chat widget, slider, tracking code and plugin adds weight. Remove anything you don't actively use and load third-party scripts only where needed.</p>"""),
  ("4. Slow or overloaded hosting", """<p>Very cheap shared hosting can be slow during busy hours. Good hosting with caching and a CDN serves pages faster, especially to visitors across India.</p>
<div class="tip"><b>Tip:</b> Test your site with Google PageSpeed Insights on mobile and fix the top suggestions first.</div>"""),
  ("5. Fonts, videos and pop-ups", """<p>Too many font weights, autoplay videos and pop-ups that cover the screen hurt speed and annoy visitors. Keep fonts to a few weights, lazy-load videos and use pop-ups sparingly.</p>"""),
  ("Why speed matters for SEO and sales", """<p>Google considers page experience, and visitors judge you in seconds. A fast site feels professional, ranks better and turns more visits into calls.</p>"""),
 ],
 takeaways=["Resize and compress images first","Use lightweight themes or custom code","Remove unused plugins and scripts","Choose good hosting with caching","Test on mobile with PageSpeed Insights"],
 faq=[("How fast should my website load?","As fast as possible — aim for the main content to appear within a couple of seconds on mobile."),("Will a faster site rank higher?","Speed is one of many factors; it helps both rankings and conversions."),("Can you speed up my existing site?","Yes. We can audit it and either optimise it or recommend a rebuild if that's more cost-effective.")]),
]

# Reading time from the real word count (about 200 words per minute)
import re as _re
for _a in ARTICLES:
    _text = _a["intro"] + " ".join(h + " " + b for h, b in _a["sections"]) + " ".join(_a["takeaways"]) + " ".join(q + " " + x for q, x in _a["faq"])
    _words = len(_re.sub(r"<[^>]+>", " ", _text).split())
    _a["words"] = _words
    _a["mins"] = f"{max(3, round(_words / 200))} min"
